      "heading": "The formula, the heads, and the mask",
      "narration": "Here is the whole thing in one line, and then we will take it apart. Attention of Q, K and V equals softmax of Q times K transpose over the square root of d-k, all multiplied by V. Four steps. Step one: Q times K transpose. Q holds one query per token as its rows, K holds one key per token, so this product computes the dot product of every query with every key at once, producing an n-by-n matrix of raw scores where entry i, j says how much token i cares about token j. Step two: divide by the square root of d-k. Step three: softmax each row, so every token's scores become weights that are positive and sum to one. Step four: multiply by V, which for each token takes the weighted average of all the value vectors. Four matrix operations, and every one of them is a GPU's favourite instruction. Now the scaling factor, because it is the one part that looks arbitrary and is not. The dot product of two d-dimensional vectors is a sum of d terms, so its typical magnitude grows with d — for a five-hundred-dimensional key, dot products are large numbers. Feed large numbers into a softmax and it saturates: it becomes almost one-hot, putting essentially all the weight on a single position. And a saturated softmax has almost no gradient, so learning through it stops. Dividing by the square root of d-k rescales the scores so their variance stays around one regardless of dimension. It is one symbol in the formula and it is the difference between the architecture training and not training. Next, multi-head attention, which is what actually gets used. Instead of computing one attention over the full dimension, split the dimension into h pieces — eight or twelve or ninety-six, depending on the model — run a separate, independent attention on each piece with its own Q, K and V matrices, then concatenate the results and pass them through one more learned projection. The total compute is roughly the same, because each head works in a smaller space. What you gain is specialization. A single attention has to commit to one notion of relevance and average everything through it. Eight heads can hold eight different notions at once, and when people examined trained models they found exactly that: heads that follow syntactic dependencies, heads that link pronouns to their referents, heads that attend to the previous token, heads that seem to do nothing at all and can be pruned. It is an ensemble of readings computed in parallel, and it is cheap. Finally, masking, which is a small implementation detail that does something enormous. Before the softmax, you can add a matrix of negative infinities to the scores, and any position you mark that way gets a weight of exactly zero. Two uses. A padding mask hides the filler tokens added to make a batch rectangular. And a causal mask hides the future: for token i, everything at position greater than i is masked out, so a token can only attend to itself and what came before it. That one triangular matrix is the entire difference between a model that reads a whole sentence at once and a model that can generate the next word — it is why BERT and GPT can share an architecture and behave completely differently.",
