      "narration": "Picture the crew before they go ashore somewhere new. Everyone is on deck, and the striking thing about that scene, if you look at it as a computation, is that every single person is doing three things simultaneously. Each of them is holding a question they need answered — Zoro wants to know what he will be fighting, Sanji wants to know what food is on the island, Nami wants to know what the weather is going to do. Each of them is also, in effect, wearing a badge that says what they are expert in: navigator, doctor, archaeologist, cook. And each of them is carrying actual knowledge they would hand over if asked. Question, badge, knowledge. Query, key, value. Same three roles, held at the same time, by everybody. Now watch how an answer actually gets formed, because this is the part people expect to work differently. Luffy asks whether it is safe to go in. He does not turn to exactly one person and take their answer whole. He reads the room — he weighs Nami's badge heavily because weather is most of the question, Robin's somewhat because she knows what the ruins mean, Chopper's a little because there was a rumour about a fever, and Usopp's not at all on this particular question. And what he ends up believing is a blend, in those proportions. That is the weighted sum, and notice that nothing was selected and nothing was thrown away. Usopp contributed a very small amount rather than zero. That softness is the difference between attention and a lookup table, and it is what makes the whole thing trainable. Now the detail that justifies having three roles instead of two, because it is easy to miss and it is the real content of the slide. Chopper's badge says doctor. That is what gets him consulted. But the thing he actually says is not the word doctor — it is a specific fact about the fever, the incubation period, whether it is airborne. The badge is how you get found; the knowledge is what you contribute. They are different objects, deliberately. If the crew could only advertise themselves using the exact content they were about to say, nobody could be consulted for a reason and then supply something unexpected, and half the value of a council would be gone. Keys and values are separate for the same reason. And one last observation about that deck. It is not one conversation. Down at one end Nami and Franky are talking about the approach and the current; in the middle Zoro and Sanji are arguing about who takes the left flank; over there Chopper is asking Robin about the writing on the rock. Several independent conversations, running in parallel, over the same group of people, each one weighting the crew differently — and then all of it is pulled together into one decision about what the crew does. That is multi-head attention exactly: the same tokens, read several different ways at once, concatenated and projected into a single output. Not a bigger conversation. Several conversations, and then a decision.",
