      "narration": "Every architecture buys something and pays for something, and it is worth being just as precise about the bill as about the benefit. Look again at what the first step computes: Q times K transpose, every query against every key. For a sequence of n tokens, that is n squared scores. Not n. n squared. And you need the whole matrix in memory to run the softmax over it. So both compute and memory grow with the square of the sequence length, and doubling your context does not double the cost, it quadruples it. Put numbers on that, because the numbers are what make it real. A thousand tokens gives you a million scores, per head, per layer — with twelve heads and twelve layers that is a hundred and forty-four million numbers just for the attention matrices, and it is fine. Ten thousand tokens gives you a hundred million per head per layer. A hundred thousand tokens gives you ten billion. That is why the context window is a headline feature that companies advertise and compete on rather than a parameter you set to whatever you like, and it is why the recurrent model's linear cost in length was not nothing — we gave up a genuinely good property to get parallelism and short paths. Now, what is actually done about it, and there are two different families with different philosophies. The first attacks the memory, and it is the more important one. FlashAttention observes that the real bottleneck is not the arithmetic — GPUs have arithmetic to spare — it is writing that enormous n-by-n matrix out to slow high-bandwidth memory and reading it back. So it restructures the computation into tiles that fit in the small, extremely fast SRAM on the chip, computes the softmax incrementally as it goes, and never materializes the full matrix at all. The mathematics is unchanged — bit for bit the same result — and it is several times faster and uses a fraction of the memory. That is why it went from a paper to being in everything within about a year, and it is a good lesson in its own right: sometimes the win is in how you move the data, not in the algorithm. The second family changes the mathematics to avoid the square. Sparse attention lets each token attend only to a chosen subset. Sliding-window attention restricts it to a local neighbourhood, sometimes with a few global tokens that everyone can see. Linear attention rewrites the operation so it never forms the n-by-n matrix in the first place. All of these are approximations — they give up some exactness — and at very long context that is obviously the right trade, because exact attention over a million tokens is not a thing you can afford. But I want to end on the balance, because it is favourable. In exchange for a quadratic bill you get both of the things the recurrent network could not give you. Every position is computed in parallel, so the hardware is finally saturated and training on enormous corpora becomes practical. And the path between any two positions is exactly one step, no matter how far apart they are, so nothing fades. Quadratic cost is a problem you can throw engineering at, and the field has. Sequential dependency and fading memory are problems you cannot.",
